1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Kong Zang 孔臧 (ca. 178–ca. 114 b.c.e.), Western Han writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kong Zang probably was born in Liaocheng 蓼城 (southwest of modern Lijin 利津, Shandong). He was the son of Kong Ju 孔聚/alt. Kong Cong 孔藂 (d. 171 b.c.e.), Marquis of Liao 蓼. When his father died, Kong Zang inherited the domain in Liao.
2 CONC  In 127 b.c.e.. Kong Zang was appointed chamberlain for ceremonies, but he was dismissed and deprived of his noble title and domain for not properly maintaining the bridge over which imperial regalia passed. The monograph on bibli
2 CONC ography of the ''Han shu'' lists his works in two sections, first in the Ruist group where he is credited with ten pieces, and second in the section on ''fu'' and songs that attributes him with twenty ''fu'' compositions. The Kong
2 CONC  Congzi 孔叢子, a work of disputed authenticity, contains four ''fu'' and two letters attributed to Kong Zang. These pieces are also of dubious authenticity.
2 CONT
2 CONT David R. Knechtges
